comments.body:
🤖 Replies to the two review notes:

@muchanem: agreed that the spill belongs in shellbox. Iris and Daytona already redirect each command's streams to files in the machine, so `SPILL_SCRIPT` wraps a second redirect around theirs; a spill option on `shellbox.machine.Command` (path prefix, per-file cap), implemented per backend, would let them keep and cap the files they already write, let Docker and local add the pipeline to the exec line they build, and let ShellSim keep the stream natively. `run_shell_call` would pass the option through, and the observation format and `tool_output_spill_bytes` would stay as they are. That touches shellbox and five backends, so it will be a follow-up rather than part of this PR.

@rjpower: agreed on separating the engine from the harness. #10165 (`ManagedAgent` = `RolloutEngine` + `RolloutSpec`) is that direction: settings such as tool sets, output spilling, and completion move from engine-level session configuration into a spec the caller supplies, with the built-in session as the default implementation.

This push also rebases onto main after #10139–#10143: spilling now lives in the shell's `ToolProviderSession`, and `tool_output_spill_bytes` is required like the other session limits.
